The `\rowcolor` command in LaTeX is used to color entire rows in tables. It's part of the `colortbl` package (which is automatically loaded by `xcolor` with the `table` option).

## Basic Syntax

```latex
\rowcolor{color}
```

## Setup

First, you need to load the required package:

```latex
\usepackage[table]{xcolor}
% or
\usepackage{colortbl}
```

## Basic Example

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[table]{xcolor}

\begin{document}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
Name & Age & City \\
\hline
\rowcolor{lightgray}
John & 25 & New York \\
\hline
Mary & 30 & Boston \\
\hline
\rowcolor{yellow}
Bob & 35 & Chicago \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{document}
```

## Color Options

```latex
% Predefined colors
\rowcolor{red}
\rowcolor{blue}
\rowcolor{green}
\rowcolor{yellow}
\rowcolor{gray}
\rowcolor{lightgray}

% Custom RGB colors
\rowcolor[RGB]{255,200,200}

% Custom colors with transparency
\rowcolor{blue!20}  % 20% blue
\rowcolor{red!50}   % 50% red
```

## Alternating Row Colors

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[table]{xcolor}

\begin{document}
\rowcolors{2}{lightgray}{white}  % Start from row 2, alternate between lightgray and white

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
Name & Age & City \\
\hline
John & 25 & New York \\
Mary & 30 & Boston \\
Bob & 35 & Chicago \\
Alice & 28 & Miami \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{document}
```

## Advanced Usage

### With Different Table Environments

```latex
% With array environment
\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{lightblue}
x & y & z \\
\hline
1 & 2 & 3 \\
\hline
\end{array}

% With longtable
\usepackage{longtable}
\begin{longtable}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{lightgray}
Header 1 & Header 2 & Header 3 \\
\hline
\endhead
Data 1 & Data 2 & Data 3 \\
\rowcolor{yellow}
More data & More data & More data \\
\end{longtable}
```

### Partial Row Coloring

```latex
% Color specific columns in a row
\rowcolor{red}
\cellcolor{blue} Cell 1 & Cell 2 & Cell 3 \\
```

## Complete Example

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[table]{xcolor}
\usepackage{booktabs}

\definecolor{headercolor}{RGB}{52, 73, 94}
\definecolor{rowcolor1}{RGB}{236, 240, 241}
\definecolor{rowcolor2}{RGB}{255, 255, 255}

\begin{document}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\rowcolor{headercolor}
\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Product}} & \textcolor{white}{\textbf{Price}} & \textcolor{white}{\textbf{Stock}} \\
\midrule
\rowcolor{rowcolor1}
Laptop & \$999 & 15 \\
\rowcolor{rowcolor2}
Mouse & \$25 & 50 \\
\rowcolor{rowcolor1}
Keyboard & \$75 & 30 \\
\rowcolor{rowcolor2}
Monitor & \$299 & 8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Product inventory with colored rows}
\end{table}

\end{document}
```

## Tips

1. **Order matters**: `\rowcolor` should be placed at the beginning of the row
2. **Combine with booktabs**: Use `\rowcolor` with `booktabs` package for professional-looking tables
3. **Performance**: For large tables, consider using `\rowcolors` instead of individual `\rowcolor` commands
4. **Accessibility**: Ensure sufficient contrast between text and background colors

The `\rowcolor` command is very useful for creating visually appealing and readable tables in LaTeX documents.

